Q.Difference  bw GZIP and ZIP
A. In SAP Cloud Integration (CPI), ZIP and GZIP are both compression methods used to reduce the size of files.
   ZIP is a general-purpose archive format that can contain multiple files, while GZIP is a compression algorithm primarily used for single files. Both have corresponding encoders and decoders for use in CPI integration flows.
   Key Differences:
   Archive vs. Single File:
   ZIP is an archive format that can hold multiple files within a single archive. GZIP, on the other hand, is used to
   compress a single file, not an archive.
   Compression Algorithm:
   ZIP uses a variety of compression algorithms, while GZIP uses the DEFLATE algorithm.
   Use Cases:
   ZIP is suitable for scenarios where you need to bundle and potentially compress multiple files together,
   like archiving documents or creating a package of data for transport. GZIP is best for optimizing the size
   of individual files, such as HTML or CSS, for web applications or when compressing a single large file.
   CPI Implementation:
   In CPI, you'll find both ZIP and GZIP encoders and decoders. The ZIP encoder can be used to create ZIP archives, while the GZIP encoder compresses a single message using the GZIP algorithm.
   In summary: ZIP is an archive format for bundling multiple files, while GZIP is a compression method for single files. In CPI, both are available for use in integration flows, depending on the specific need to archive or compress individual messages.
