Q. In Solace, exclusive queues allow only one consumer to bind and receive messages at a time,
   acting like a "active/standby" model. Non-exclusive queues, on the other hand, permit multiple
   consumers to bind, enabling load balancing and message distribution across consumers.

   Exclusive Queues:

   Single Active Consumer:
   Only one consumer can be bound to the queue and actively receiving messages. Other consumers can
   bind but will be in standby mode until the active consumer unbinds.

   Active/Standby:
   This model is useful for scenarios where a single consumer is responsible for processing messages
   and a standby consumer is ready to take over if the active consumer fails.

   Ordered Delivery:
   Exclusive queues guarantee that messages are delivered in the order they were received.

   Non-Exclusive Queues:

   Multiple Consumers:
   Multiple consumers can bind to the queue and receive messages.

   Round-Robin Distribution:
   Messages are distributed to consumers in a round-robin manner, or based on the speed of each consumer.

   Load Balancing and Auto-Scaling:
   Non-exclusive queues are suitable for load balancing and enabling dynamic scaling of consumers to match the workload.

   Non-Ordered Delivery (Potentially):
   In some cases, depending on the configuration (especially with partitioned queues), messages might be delivered to consumers out of order, especially if a connection fails.

   In summary: Exclusive queues are for scenarios requiring a single active consumer, while
   non-exclusive queues are for scenarios requiring multiple consumers and load balancing.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
