Q. when can we use graphical , xslt , groovy in mappings?
Graphical, XSLT, and Groovy mappings offer different approaches to transforming data between various
formats. Graphical mapping is suitable for simple transformations, while XSLT and Groovy are better
suited for complex logic and custom code. Choosing the right tool depends on the complexity and
specific requirements of the mapping task.

Graphical Mapping:
Use cases: Simple data mapping between XML formats, straightforward transformations.
Advantages: Visual and user-friendly, suitable for beginners, good for simple transformations.
Limitations: Can become cumbersome for complex logic.

XSLT Mapping:
Use cases:
Transforming XML data, filtering, sorting, and grouping data, generating non-XML outputs like text or HTML.
Advantages:
Powerful for XML transformations, provides XPath functions, can be used with ABAP and Java extensions.
Limitations:
Can become complex for very large messages or when needing to access external resources or Java APIs.

Groovy Mapping:
Use cases:
Complex logic, custom code execution, accessing external resources or Java APIs, value mapping, ensuring data precision.
Advantages:
More flexible and powerful than XSLT, allows for custom code, can handle complex scenarios.
Limitations:
Requires programming knowledge, can be less efficient than other mapping methods for simple transformations.

In summary:
Simple mappings: Use graphical mapping for basic XML-to-XML transformations.
Complex XML transformations: Use XSLT for filtering, sorting, grouping, and generating non-XML outputs.
Complex logic and custom code: Use Groovy for complex logic, custom code execution, and accessing external resources.
Performance: Avoid XSLT for very large messages to prevent performance issues.
Maintainability: Choose the mapping approach that is easiest to understand and maintain for the specific scenario.
